## Why This Question Matters
Provides a practical, beginner-friendly approach to assessing grounding, code fidelity, and relevance for a developer assistant across private documentation corpora with versioning support.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding responses to exact doc version and section
- Detection of code block hallucinations
- Metrics: precision@1, grounding coverage, and citation drift

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal runner skeleton
const prompts = loadPrompts('prompts-50.json');
prompts.forEach(p => {
  const ans = model.ask(p);
  const grounded = verifyGrounding(ans, p.docVersion, p.section);
  const hallucinated = detectHallucinations(ans, p.docVersion);

  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    prompt: p.question,
    grounded,
    hallucinated,
    precisionAt1: computePrecision(ans, p.expectedEndpoints)
  }));
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Enterprise chat assistants must process multilingual, long-context inputs containing specialized domain terminology while maintaining performance. This question evaluates understanding of tokenizer design, attention mechanisms, and practical engineering trade-offs in production systems.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual subword tokenization with domain-specific vocabulary enhancement
- Sparse attention patterns using local windows and global anchors
- Cross-language semantic alignment and retrieval augmentation
- Latency-accuracy trade-offs in large-scale deployment

## Code Example
```javascript
function buildSparseMask(n, localWin, globalAnchors) {
  const mask = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  // Local attention windows
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const start = Math.max(0, i - localWin);
    const end = Math.min(n, i + localWin + 1);
    for (let j = start; j < end; j++) {
      mask[i][j] = true;
    }
  }

  // Global anchor attention
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (const anchor of globalAnchors) {
      mask[i][anchor] = true;
      mask[anchor][i] = true;
    }
  }

  return mask;
}
```

